  "tips": [
    {
      "id": "a1-u01-d05-t01",
      "before": "a1-u01-d05-e01",
      "title": { "en": "ś and ć" },
      "body": { "en": "*Ś* and *ć* are soft versions of *sz* and *cz*. Say them with the middle of the tongue against the roof of the mouth and the lips spread, as in a smile. *Cześć* has all three sounds: *cz*, *ś*, *ć*." }
    },
    {
      "id": "a1-u01-d05-t02",
      "before": "a1-u01-d05-e07",
      "title": { "en": "si and ci" },
      "body": { "en": "Before a vowel, *si* is said like *ś* and *ci* like *ć*, and the *i* itself is not heard: *się* is *ś* followed by *ę*. *Jak się masz?* and *co słychać?* both ask how someone is. *Co słychać?* is the more everyday one." }
    }
  ],
